-- Six launch articles for the blog. Generated by scripts/build-blog-seed.mjs from supabase/blog/*.md.
-- Run once in the Supabase SQL Editor (after supabase/schema.sql). Running it again refreshes the text of these six posts.
-- Cover images are served from the website itself (/img/blog/...), so no upload is needed.

insert into public.blog_posts (title, slug, excerpt, content, cover_image, category, tags, seo_description, author_name, status, published_at)
values
('AI Features for Mobile Apps: 6 Practical Uses That Help Users', 'ai-features-for-mobile-apps', 'AI works best in an app when it saves a user a specific amount of effort. Here are six practical features, how they work and what to check before you build them.', $md$AI features can make a mobile app feel built for each person who opens it. They can also add cost, delay and risk if they are added for their own sake. The useful ones share a pattern: they remove a specific chore or make a specific decision easier.

This guide covers six AI features we see working in mobile apps, with the questions to answer before you build each one. We build AI into products in three main ways: personalization, task automation and behavior prediction. Most of the examples below fall into one of those groups.

## 1. Personalized content and recommendations

Personalization changes what each user sees based on what they do. A fitness app reorders workouts around what a user completes. A learning app picks the next lesson from the user's mistakes. A marketplace shows listings that match past searches.

**When it helps:** your app has more content or options than one person will browse, and users have different goals.

**What to check:** you need enough usage data to make good suggestions. Start with simple rules, such as "recently used" and "most relevant," and add machine learning once you can measure that suggestions improve results.

## 2. Smart input and task automation

Typing on a phone is slow. AI can reduce it by filling in fields, sorting entries into categories, reading text from photos and turning a short note into a structured record.

Examples include scanning a receipt to create an expense, suggesting a category for a new task and turning a voice note into a checklist.

**When it helps:** your users repeat the same data entry many times a day.

**What to check:** always let users correct the result. A wrong suggestion that is hard to fix frustrates more than no suggestion.

## 3. Behavior prediction and timely nudges

Prediction uses past behavior to estimate what a user is likely to do next. An app can send a reminder at the time a person usually completes a task, flag users who are drifting away or suggest the next action in a routine.

**When it helps:** timing matters, such as habits, appointments, renewals and restocking.

**What to check:** nudges must stay useful. Too many notifications push users to turn them off or delete the app. Give users control over frequency, and measure whether a nudge led to a useful action, not only whether it was opened.

## 4. Natural language search and commands

Users often know what they want but not what the app calls it. Natural language search lets them type "invoices from last month for the Lopez account" instead of building a filter.

**When it helps:** your app holds a lot of structured data, such as records, documents or transactions.

**What to check:** test with real phrases from your audience. Niche users have specialized vocabulary, and a general model may misread it unless you provide the terms.

## 5. Summaries and drafting

AI can summarize long content, such as meeting notes, customer messages or reports, and can draft replies for the user to review. This saves time in apps where people read and write a lot.

**When it helps:** the user's job involves repetitive writing or reading.

**What to check:** keep a human in the loop for anything sent to others. Show the draft, let the user edit and make clear what the app generated.

## 6. A support assistant inside the app

An in-app assistant answers common questions using your own help content. It reduces support load and helps users get unstuck without leaving the app.

**When it helps:** your app has enough features that users regularly ask "how do I...?"

**What to check:** limit the assistant to your documentation and make it easy to reach a person. A confident wrong answer damages trust.

## What to check before adding any AI feature

- **Is the problem real?** Confirm through user research that the chore exists. Our guide to [niche market research](/blog/niche-market-research-for-app-ideas) shows how.
- **Does it need AI?** Some problems are solved by a good default or a simple rule. Use AI where rules cannot cope.
- **How will you handle data and privacy?** Decide what data the feature uses, where it is processed and what users are told. Follow the laws that apply to your audience.
- **What happens when it is wrong?** Plan for errors, with correction paths and fallbacks.
- **What will it cost to run?** AI calls have ongoing costs that grow with usage. Include them in your budget, as described in [what drives app costs](/blog/how-much-does-it-cost-to-build-a-mobile-app).
- **How will you measure it?** Pick one result, such as time saved or tasks completed, and compare before and after.

## Start small

Add one AI feature to a first version that already does its main job well. Measure it, improve it and then decide whether to add another. A focused app with one well-chosen feature beats an app with many half-working ones. If you are still defining that first version, start with [how to build a niche mobile app](/blog/how-to-build-a-niche-mobile-app).

## Frequently asked questions

### Do I need a lot of data to use AI in my app?

Not for every feature. Smart input, summaries and search can work with pre-trained models. Personalization and prediction improve as you collect usage data.

### Will AI features slow down my app?

They can if they run on every screen load. Run them in the background where possible, cache results and show the interface first.

### Is AI worth it for a small niche audience?

Often yes, because a small audience has specific needs that general apps ignore. The key is choosing a feature that solves one of those needs.

## Thinking about AI for your app?

We help teams decide which AI features are worth building and which are not. [Tell us about your app](/#contact) and we will suggest where AI can help your users.$md$, '/img/blog/ai-features-for-mobile-apps.jpg', 'AI', array['AI in mobile apps', 'personalization', 'task automation', 'behavior prediction'], 'Six practical AI features for mobile apps, from personalization to task automation and behavior prediction, plus what to check before you add them.', 'Digital Legends Team', 'published', now() - interval '22 days'),
('App Store Optimization for Niche Apps: A Practical Checklist', 'app-store-optimization-for-niche-apps', 'App store optimization helps the right people find and install your app. This checklist covers keywords, listing text, visuals, ratings and measurement for niche apps.', $md$App store optimization, or ASO, is the work of making your app easy to find in the App Store and Google Play and easy to choose once someone lands on your page. For a niche app it matters even more than for a mass-market one, because your users search with specific words and you cannot outspend large competitors on ads.

This checklist covers the parts of ASO that you control: keywords, listing text, visuals, ratings and measurement. Stores change their rules and limits over time, so check the current guidelines from Apple and Google before you finalize your listing.

## What ASO does for a niche app

People discover apps in two main ways: they search, or they browse features and charts. Niche apps win through search. Someone who types "route planner for mobile dog groomers" has a clear need, and an app that matches those words can reach them with no advertising.

ASO also improves conversion. A clear listing turns more visitors into installs, which in turn can help your ranking.

## Step 1: Choose keywords your audience actually uses

Start with the language you collected in your [market research](/blog/niche-market-research-for-app-ideas). Write down the phrases people use to describe the problem, the task and the tools they use today.

Then sort them:

- **Specific phrases** that describe your niche and task. These are your best targets, since fewer apps compete for them.
- **Broader category terms** such as "scheduling" or "invoicing." They bring more searches but more competition.
- **Competitor names.** Be careful here. Stores restrict using other brands' names in your listing.

Check the search results for each term. If the first page is full of unrelated apps, the term has little competition and may suit you. If it shows apps for your exact audience, study how they describe themselves.

## Step 2: Write a title and subtitle that match intent

Your app name and the short text beside it carry the most weight in search and are the first thing people read. Put your most important keyword in the name or subtitle and make the benefit clear.

Good listings answer two questions at a glance: what is this, and who is it for? A name followed by a plain description, such as a tool name plus the job it does for the audience, usually beats a clever name alone.

Keep the text readable. Stuffing keywords makes the listing look spammy and can violate store rules.

## Step 3: Use the description to convince, not only to rank

On Google Play, the description is indexed for search, so use your keywords naturally in readable sentences. On the App Store, the description does not affect search ranking the same way, but it still helps people decide.

Write for the visitor:

- Lead with the main benefit in the first lines.
- Describe the tasks your audience does, in their vocabulary.
- Mention the features that matter most, in a short list.
- Say who the app is for. A niche app should state its niche clearly.

## Step 4: Design screenshots and previews that explain the app

Most people decide from the screenshots and the first lines of text. Use the screenshot space to tell a story:

- The first screenshot should show the main benefit and the main screen.
- Add a short caption in the audience's language to each screenshot.
- Show real use, with the interface in a realistic state.
- If a short preview video helps explain the workflow, add one.

Update visuals when your interface changes, so the store page matches what people get.

## Step 5: Earn ratings and reviews at the right moment

Ratings affect both ranking and trust. Ask for a review after a user has completed the main task successfully, not on first launch or in the middle of something. Use the official in-app review prompts that the platforms provide, and respect their limits on how often you can ask.

Reply to reviews, especially critical ones. A calm, helpful reply shows prospective users that someone is looking after the app. Fix the problems that appear repeatedly, because they often point to the next improvement.

## Step 6: Localize if your niche spans regions

If your audience works in several languages or countries, translate the listing, not only the app. Localized titles, keywords and screenshots can open search traffic that competitors ignore. Start with the one or two languages where you already have users.

## Step 7: Measure and improve

Both stores give you data on impressions, page views and installs. Watch three numbers:

1. **Impressions to page views.** Shows whether your title and icon attract clicks in search.
2. **Page views to installs.** Shows whether the page convinces visitors.
3. **Installs to active users.** Shows whether the right people are installing.

Change one thing at a time, such as the first screenshot or the subtitle, and compare results over a few weeks. Both stores offer tools for testing listing variations.

## Go beyond the stores

ASO works best with other channels pointing at your listing. A page on your website that explains the app and links to the stores, articles that answer your audience's questions and posts in the communities where your users gather all send people who are ready to install. Your launch plan from [how to build a niche mobile app](/blog/how-to-build-a-niche-mobile-app) should include these from the start.

## ASO checklist

- [ ] List the phrases your audience uses
- [ ] Check competition for each phrase
- [ ] Put the main keyword in the name or subtitle
- [ ] Write a description that leads with the benefit
- [ ] Add captions to screenshots that show the main task
- [ ] Ask for reviews after a success, and reply to feedback
- [ ] Localize for your top languages
- [ ] Track impressions, page views and installs monthly

## Frequently asked questions

### How long does ASO take to work?

Small changes can show up within days or weeks, but ranking shifts build over time. Review your results monthly rather than daily.

### Do I need paid ads for a niche app?

Not at the start. Search and community channels can bring a small, well-matched audience. Paid ads can help later, once you know your conversion rate and what an install is worth.

### How often should I update my listing?

Review it every time you ship a major feature, and at least a few times a year. Keep keywords, screenshots and text in step with the app.

## Need help launching your app?

We plan the store listing and launch together with the build. [Contact our team](/#contact) to talk about your app and your audience.$md$, '/img/blog/app-store-optimization-for-niche-apps.jpg', 'Marketing', array['ASO', 'app store optimization', 'app marketing', 'niche apps'], 'A practical app store optimization checklist for niche apps: choose keywords, write your listing, design screenshots, earn reviews and measure results.', 'Digital Legends Team', 'published', now() - interval '8 days'),
('What Does It Cost to Build a Mobile App? Key Price Drivers', 'how-much-does-it-cost-to-build-a-mobile-app', 'There is no single price for a mobile app. The cost depends on scope, platforms, design, integrations and more. Here is what moves the number and how to control it.', $md$The honest answer to "how much does a mobile app cost?" is "it depends on what you build." Two apps that look similar on a phone can need very different amounts of work behind the screen. Anyone who quotes a price without asking questions is guessing.

You can still plan. Costs follow a handful of drivers, and once you know them you can shape the project to fit a budget. This guide explains the main drivers, the costs that appear after launch and how to get quotes you can compare.

## Why there is no single price

An app is a set of decisions: what it does, on which devices, how it looks, what it connects to and how it is kept running. Each decision adds or removes work. A simple app that displays information needs far less effort than one with accounts, payments, live data and an admin dashboard.

Rather than asking for a price, ask for an estimate for a clearly defined scope. The rest of this guide helps you define it.

## The main cost drivers

### 1. Features and complexity

Every feature costs design, development, testing and maintenance. Features that look small can hide a lot of work. "Users can log in" includes sign-up, password reset, error states, security and account deletion.

### 2. Platforms

Building for iOS, Android or both changes the scope. Cross-platform tools let one codebase serve both and can reduce effort. Native development for each platform gives the most control over performance and device features but takes more work. Your audience's devices should guide the choice. Our comparison of [mobile app vs web app](/blog/mobile-app-vs-web-app-which-to-build-first) covers the decision from another angle.

### 3. Design depth

A clean interface built from standard components costs less than custom illustrations, animations and a bespoke design system. Good design is not optional, because it affects whether people finish the main task, but you can choose how far to take it in version one.

### 4. Backend and integrations

Most apps need a server side: a database, user accounts, file storage and an admin area. Integrations with payment providers, maps, calendars, messaging or other software add work and ongoing fees. Each outside service also has its own rules and failure modes to handle.

### 5. AI features

AI features add design, data handling, testing and running costs, since model usage is billed by volume. They can be worth it when they save users real effort. See [AI features for mobile apps](/blog/ai-features-for-mobile-apps) for how to judge that.

### 6. Security, privacy and compliance

Apps that handle payments, health information or children's data carry extra requirements. Meeting them takes planning and testing, and it is far cheaper to design for them from the start than to add them later.

### 7. Team and location

Rates differ between freelancers, agencies and in-house teams, and between regions. A cheaper rate does not always mean a cheaper project if it leads to rework. Look at experience with projects like yours, and at how clearly the team communicates.

### 8. Timeline

Tight deadlines often need more people working in parallel, which raises cost. A realistic schedule with a clear scope is usually the most economical.

## Costs that appear after launch

The build is not the whole budget. Plan for:

- **Store fees.** Apple and Google charge developer account fees.
- **Hosting and services.** Servers, databases, messaging and analytics tools bill monthly.
- **Maintenance.** Operating systems and devices change every year, and libraries need updates and security fixes.
- **Support and improvements.** Real users will find problems and ask for features.
- **Marketing.** An app does not find users on its own. See [app store optimization for niche apps](/blog/app-store-optimization-for-niche-apps).

A common planning habit is to set aside a share of the build budget every year for upkeep. Ask any team you work with what their maintenance arrangement looks like.

## How to keep the cost under control

1. **Build a first version that does one job.** Cut every feature that is not needed for the main task. [How to build a niche mobile app](/blog/how-to-build-a-niche-mobile-app) shows how to choose that job.
2. **Do discovery first.** A short planning phase of research, wireframes and a feature list turns a vague idea into a scope you can price.
3. **Work in phases.** Launch, learn from users and fund the next phase from what you learned.
4. **Reuse proven components.** Standard login, payment and notification services are cheaper and more reliable than custom builds.
5. **Decide what can wait.** An admin dashboard can start simple. Advanced analytics and extra languages can come later.

## How to get quotes you can compare

Send every team the same brief. Include:

- The audience and the problem the app solves
- The core features, in priority order
- Target platforms
- Integrations you already know you need
- Your timeline and budget range
- What "done" means for version one

Then compare quotes on what they include, not only the total. Check who handles design, testing, project management and launch, and what happens after delivery.

## Red flags

- A fixed price with no questions about your goals
- No written scope or list of what is excluded
- No mention of testing, security or maintenance
- Pressure to decide quickly
- Reluctance to show similar work or explain their process

## Frequently asked questions

### Is a cheaper app a bad idea?

Not necessarily. A small, focused app can be a smart first step. The risk is paying for a low price up front and then rebuilding because the foundation was weak.

### Can I start with a prototype?

Yes. A clickable prototype lets you test the idea with real users for a small fraction of the cost of a full build.

### Does a niche app cost less than a mass-market app?

Often the scope is smaller, because you serve one audience's main needs. The effort still depends on features and integrations, not on the size of the audience.

## Get an estimate for your project

At Digital Legends we start with discovery so that you get an estimate based on a real scope. [Share your idea with us](/#contact) and we will explain what it would take.$md$, '/img/blog/how-much-does-it-cost-to-build-a-mobile-app.jpg', 'Guides', array['app development cost', 'mobile app budget', 'MVP', 'project scoping'], 'What does it cost to build a mobile app? Learn the factors that drive price, the hidden costs after launch and how to get comparable quotes.', 'Digital Legends Team', 'published', now() - interval '15 days'),
('How to Build a Niche Mobile App: A Step-by-Step Guide', 'how-to-build-a-niche-mobile-app', 'A niche mobile app serves one specific group of people extremely well. Here are the six steps we follow, from choosing the audience to launching the first version.', $md$A niche mobile app is built for one specific group of people with one specific need. It does not try to serve everyone. A scheduling app for physiotherapists, a route planner for mobile pet groomers and a logbook for hobby beekeepers are all niche apps.

Building one works differently from building a mass-market app. You have a smaller audience, so every feature has to earn its place, and you can talk to your future users directly. This guide walks through the six steps we use when we build niche mobile apps, and the mistakes that cost founders the most time.

## What counts as a niche mobile app?

A niche app has three traits:

- **A narrow audience.** You can describe the user in one sentence, including their job, situation or hobby.
- **A sharp problem.** The app removes a specific frustration that general-purpose tools handle badly.
- **Fewer competitors with real focus.** Large apps may touch the problem, but nobody builds around it.

If your audience description needs the word "everyone," the idea is not niche yet.

## Step 1: Pick one audience you can describe in a sentence

Start with the person, not the feature list. Write a sentence like this: "Independent physiotherapists who run their own clinic and manage bookings by phone and text."

That sentence tells you where to find these people, what their day looks like and what words they use. If you cannot write it, narrow the audience until you can. You can always widen later. Narrowing after launch is much harder.

## Step 2: Learn how they solve the problem today

Every niche already has a workaround. It might be a spreadsheet, a group chat, a paper notebook or three apps stitched together. Your first job is to find it.

Talk to at least five people in your audience. Ask what they did the last time the problem came up, which tools they used and what annoyed them. Past behavior tells you more than opinions about a hypothetical app. Our guide to [niche market research for app ideas](/blog/niche-market-research-for-app-ideas) covers the questions to ask and how to read the answers.

## Step 3: Define a first version that does one job

The first version of a niche app should do one job well. Pick the single task your audience repeats most often and make that task faster or easier than their current workaround.

A useful test: describe the app in one line, "It lets X do Y without Z." If the line needs the word "and" twice, cut features. A smaller first version ships sooner, costs less and gives you real feedback. Our breakdown of [what drives the cost of a mobile app](/blog/how-much-does-it-cost-to-build-a-mobile-app) shows how each extra feature changes the budget.

## Step 4: Design for the context your users are in

Niche users often work in specific conditions. A beekeeper wears gloves in a field with weak signal. A physiotherapist checks bookings between patients. Design for those moments:

- Make the main action reachable in one or two taps.
- Support offline use when your audience works without reliable signal.
- Use the vocabulary your audience uses, not generic labels.
- Keep onboarding short. Ask only for what the first task needs.

This is also where you decide where AI helps. Personalization, task automation and behavior prediction can make a focused app feel tailored, as long as the basics already work. We cover this in [AI features for mobile apps](/blog/ai-features-for-mobile-apps).

## Step 5: Build, test with real users and fix before launch

Build the first version in short cycles and put each one in front of real users. Give five to ten people from your audience early builds and watch them use the app. Note where they hesitate, what they skip and what they ask about.

Fix the problems that block the main task first. Polish and secondary features can wait. Test on real devices, including older phones that your audience may still use, and check performance on a weak connection.

## Step 6: Launch where your audience already is

A niche audience gathers in specific places: forums, social groups, newsletters, trade events and professional associations. Launch there before you spend anything on broad advertising.

Prepare your store listing with the words your audience searches for. [App store optimization for niche apps](/blog/app-store-optimization-for-niche-apps) explains how to choose keywords and write a listing that converts. Ask your first users for reviews once they have completed the main task, not at random moments.

## Common mistakes to avoid

- **Building for a broad audience "to be safe."** A vague audience produces a vague app.
- **Adding features before the main task works.** Each addition delays launch and adds maintenance.
- **Skipping user conversations.** Assumptions about niche users are often wrong, because you are not always one of them.
- **Ignoring maintenance.** Operating systems and devices change. Plan for updates after launch.
- **Choosing the platform by habit.** Think about whether you need a mobile app first or a web app. Our comparison of [mobile app vs web app](/blog/mobile-app-vs-web-app-which-to-build-first) helps you decide.

## Frequently asked questions

### How long does it take to build a niche mobile app?

It depends on the scope. A focused first version that does one job takes less time than an app with accounts, payments and several user roles. A short discovery phase gives you a realistic timeline before you commit.

### Do I need to build for both iOS and Android?

Not always. If your audience mostly uses one platform, start there. Cross-platform tools can also cover both with one codebase. Ask your audience which phones they use before you decide.

### How do I know if my niche is big enough?

Count the reachable people, not the theoretical market. A small group that has a painful problem and pays for solutions can support a healthy product. Interviews and a simple waiting list will tell you more than a market-size estimate.

## Ready to build for your niche?

Digital Legends designs and builds mobile apps for specialized audiences, from first idea to launch. [Tell us about your project](/#contact) and who it is for, and we will tell you how we would build it.$md$, '/img/blog/how-to-build-a-niche-mobile-app.jpg', 'Guides', array['niche apps', 'mobile app development', 'app MVP', 'startup'], 'Learn how to build a niche mobile app in six steps: choose an audience, validate the idea, scope a first version, design, test and launch.', 'Digital Legends Team', 'published', now() - interval '36 days'),
('Mobile App vs Web App: Which Should You Build First?', 'mobile-app-vs-web-app-which-to-build-first', 'Should your product start as a mobile app or a web app? The answer depends on how people use it. Here is how to decide, with a simple comparison and a hybrid path.', $md$Founders often ask whether to build a mobile app or a web app first. Both can serve the same users, but they behave differently and cost different amounts of effort. Choosing well saves months, and choosing by habit can send you down an expensive path.

This guide compares the two, lists the situations where each wins and describes a hybrid route that many teams use.

## The short definitions

- **Mobile app:** installed from an app store, runs on the phone and can use device features such as the camera, notifications and sensors.
- **Web app:** runs in a browser at a web address, works on any device with a browser and needs no installation.
- **Progressive web app (PWA):** a web app with extra abilities, such as working offline and being added to the home screen. It sits between the two.

## Comparison at a glance

| Question | Mobile app | Web app |
| --- | --- | --- |
| How do users get it? | Download from a store | Open a link |
| Works offline? | Yes, by design | Possible with a PWA |
| Device features (camera, sensors, push) | Full access | Limited, varies by browser |
| Discovery | App stores, ads, referrals | Search engines, links, ads |
| Updates | Users install updates, store review | Instant for everyone |
| Development effort | Platform-specific work or cross-platform tools | One codebase for all devices |
| Best for | Frequent, personal, device-linked use | Occasional use, wide reach, content and dashboards |

## When to build a mobile app first

Choose mobile first when:

- **Users return often.** Daily habits such as tracking, messaging or logging benefit from a home screen icon and quick launch.
- **You need device features.** Camera scanning, location, Bluetooth, health data and reliable push notifications work best in a native or cross-platform app.
- **Offline use matters.** Field workers, travelers and people in areas with weak signal need an app that works without a connection.
- **The experience should feel personal.** Notifications at the right moment and instant access make an app part of someone's routine.

Many niche products fit here, since niche audiences often use specific tools in specific situations on their phones. Our guide to [building a niche mobile app](/blog/how-to-build-a-niche-mobile-app) walks through the process.

## When to build a web app first

Choose web first when:

- **Users visit occasionally.** If people use the product weekly or monthly, asking them to install something adds friction.
- **Reach matters most.** A link works on every device immediately, with no store approval.
- **Search is your main channel.** Web pages can be found by search engines. Apps are found mainly through the stores.
- **The product is mostly screens of data or forms.** Dashboards, admin tools and booking pages work well in a browser.
- **You need to change things quickly.** Web updates go live for everyone at once.

## What about cost and maintenance?

A web app has one codebase for all devices, which usually means less work to start. A mobile app adds store accounts, review processes, device testing and regular updates as operating systems change. Cross-platform tools reduce duplicate work, but you still plan for testing on real devices.

Costs depend on scope more than on the platform alone. Our article on [what drives the cost of a mobile app](/blog/how-much-does-it-cost-to-build-a-mobile-app) explains the factors.

## A hybrid path that many teams use

You do not have to pick one forever. A common route is:

1. **Validate with a web app or PWA.** Launch a simple version that proves people want the product.
2. **Learn from real usage.** See which tasks people repeat and which features they ask for.
3. **Build the mobile app once the use is clear.** Move the most frequent tasks to a mobile app and reuse the backend.

The reverse also works. If you are sure your audience lives on their phones, start with mobile and add a web version for administration and sign-up pages later. Share one backend so data stays in one place.

## Questions to ask yourself

- How often will people use this?
- Does the main task need the camera, location, notifications or offline access?
- Where will my audience find the product, in search or in a store?
- How quickly do I need to launch and change things?
- What devices does my audience use? Ask them, as covered in [niche market research](/blog/niche-market-research-for-app-ideas).

If most answers point to frequent, personal, device-linked use, start with mobile. If they point to occasional use, wide reach and search discovery, start with web.

## Frequently asked questions

### Is a PWA a good compromise?

For many products, yes. A PWA gives you installability and some offline support from one web codebase. Check that the features you need are supported on the browsers your audience uses, especially on iPhones.

### Can I build both at the same time?

You can, but it multiplies effort. Most teams get better results by launching one, learning and then adding the other.

### Which is better for AI features?

Both can host them. AI features usually run on a server, so the choice depends more on how users interact than on the AI itself. See [AI features for mobile apps](/blog/ai-features-for-mobile-apps) for ideas.

## Not sure which to start with?

We help teams choose the right first platform based on their audience and goals. [Describe your product to us](/#contact) and we will recommend a starting point.$md$, '/img/blog/mobile-app-vs-web-app-which-to-build-first.jpg', 'Product', array['mobile app vs web app', 'PWA', 'product strategy', 'app development'], 'Mobile app vs web app: compare reach, features, cost and maintenance, and learn how to choose what to build first, including a hybrid PWA path.', 'Digital Legends Team', 'published', now() - interval '1 days'),
('Niche Market Research for App Ideas: A Practical Guide', 'niche-market-research-for-app-ideas', 'Good research tells you what your audience already does, what frustrates them and whether they would switch. This guide shows how to find out before you build.', $md$Most app ideas fail because nobody checked whether people wanted them, not because the code was bad. Niche market research is how you check. It takes days or weeks, costs very little compared with development and can save months of building the wrong thing.

This guide covers the research we run before we design a niche app: how to define the audience, where to find them, what to ask, how to read competitor reviews and how to decide whether to move forward.

## Why research comes before design

When your audience is small, a wrong guess is expensive. You cannot recover from a poor launch by sending more traffic, because there is only so much traffic in a niche. Research reduces the guessing. It shows you which problems are painful enough that people pay to solve them, and which are mild annoyances nobody will download an app for.

It also gives you the language of your audience. The words people use to describe their problem become your app store listing, your onboarding text and your website copy.

## Step 1: Define the niche precisely

Write down who the user is, what they do and in what situation the problem appears. "Small business owners" is too broad. "Owners of single-location bakeries who take custom orders by message" is a niche.

Add boundaries: where they live or work, how technical they are, what devices they use. These details decide where you recruit people for interviews and which channels you can use later.

## Step 2: Find where your audience talks

People in a niche gather in predictable places:

- Online forums and subreddits about the activity or profession
- Facebook, LinkedIn and Discord groups
- Trade associations, newsletters and podcasts
- Review sections of the tools they already use
- Local meetups, courses and trade shows

Read before you post. Look for repeated complaints, workarounds people share and questions that come up again and again. Save the exact phrases. Patterns across many threads are stronger evidence than one loud comment.

## Step 3: Interview five to ten people

Interviews give you the depth that surveys miss. Aim for five to ten conversations of about thirty minutes with people who match your niche definition.

Ask about the past, not the future:

- "Tell me about the last time you had to [do the task]. What happened?"
- "What tools did you use? What did you do when they didn't work?"
- "What is the most annoying part of this?"
- "Have you tried to fix it? What did you try?"
- "What does it cost you in time or money when it goes wrong?"

Avoid pitching your idea in the first half of the call. People are polite and will say a hypothetical app sounds great. What they did last week is more reliable than what they say they would do.

## Step 4: Study competitors and their reviews

List every tool your audience uses for the problem, including spreadsheets, paper and general apps. Then read the one- and two-star reviews of the closest apps. They show what frustrates users in their own words.

Look for:

- Features people ask for that no app provides
- Complaints about complexity, price or missing support
- Workflows the apps force on users that do not match real work

Gaps that appear across many reviews point to a product opportunity. Note that a crowded category is not always bad news. It proves demand. The question is whether anyone serves your specific niche well.

## Step 5: Check whether the niche can support a product

You do not need a precise market size. You need an honest answer to three questions:

1. **Can you reach these people?** You should be able to name three places to find them.
2. **Is the problem frequent or costly?** Daily irritation and expensive mistakes justify a product. Rare inconveniences do not.
3. **Do they already pay for solutions?** People who pay for tools, services or workarounds will usually pay for a better one.

A simple test is a landing page that describes the app and collects email addresses. If you can drive a small group from your niche to sign up, you have a signal.

## Step 6: Turn findings into decisions

Summarize your research on one page:

- The audience in one sentence
- The top three problems, in the audience's words
- How they solve each problem today
- The one problem your first version will solve
- What would make you stop

That last item matters. Decide in advance what evidence would convince you the idea is not worth building. It protects you from falling in love with a weak idea.

Once you have a clear problem, you can scope a first version. Our guide to [building a niche mobile app](/blog/how-to-build-a-niche-mobile-app) continues from here, and the post on [what a mobile app costs](/blog/how-much-does-it-cost-to-build-a-mobile-app) helps you set a budget around that scope.

## Frequently asked questions

### How many interviews are enough?

Most founders hear the same problems repeated after five to eight conversations. Keep going until new interviews stop surprising you.

### What if I cannot find anyone to interview?

That is also research. If you cannot find or reach people in the niche, marketing the app later will be just as hard. Try a different angle on the audience or a channel where they gather.

### Should I use surveys instead?

Surveys work well after interviews, when you know what to ask and want to measure how common a problem is. They are weak for discovering problems you have not thought of.

## Want a second pair of eyes on your idea?

